\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{The sign torus and its frame factor}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{Let #{G} be the group with two elements, acting on the orbit of
one sign. The stabilizer is trivial, so the orbit is free and has
two points. The labeled torus of \ref{fdip-010H} has #{4} vertices.
\ref{fdip-0124} therefore multiplies the reduced sum by #{2^4=16}.
The reduced sum in \ref{fdip-0110} is #{34416}. The product is the
joint sum of \ref{fdip-010H}:
##{16\times 34416
 =10\times 34416+6\times 34416
 =344160+206496
 =550656.}
}
